# moz-official -- COMMON half (LW-M1-04).
#
# Split out of patches/moz-official.patch, a straddler: it mixed two core GMP
# hunks that are compiled on every target with three browser/base hunks that
# only exist in a desktop build. The browser/ half now lives in
# patches/moz-official-desktop.patch. common + desktop together are, byte for
# byte, the hunks of the original patch, in the original order within each half.
#
# What is here:
#   dom/media/gmp/ChromiumCDMAdapter.cpp     2 hunks, #ifdef MOZILLA_OFFICIAL -> #if 0
#   media/gmp-clearkey/0.1/gmp-clearkey.cpp  1 hunk,  #ifdef MOZILLA_OFFICIAL -> #if 0
#
# What it does: disables CDM *host file verification*. A build with
# MOZILLA_OFFICIAL set expects signed host files (the executable and libxul) and
# refuses to initialise the CDM adapter, or to create a clearkey CDM instance,
# when it cannot read and verify them. LibreWolf is an unofficial rebuild, so
# those signatures never exist; `#if 0` switches the checks off rather than
# switching MOZILLA_OFFICIAL off, which would change dozens of unrelated things.
#
# Why it is common -- proved from the pristine firefox-153.0.4 tree, not from
# the path:
#   * dom/media/moz.build:28-36 puts "gmp" in an unconditional DIRS, and
#     dom/media/gmp/moz.build:65-67 lists ChromiumCDMAdapter.cpp in
#     UNIFIED_SOURCES with no guard above it (the first `if CONFIG[...]` in that
#     file is at :123, well below). It compiles on Android.
#   * toolkit/toolkit.mozbuild:167-169 does `DIRS += ["/media/gmp-clearkey/0.1"]`
#     unconditionally, and media/gmp-clearkey/0.1/moz.build:22-25 lists
#     gmp-clearkey.cpp in UNIFIED_SOURCES. That same moz.build has an explicit
#     Android branch at :13-16 -- `if CONFIG["OS_TARGET"] == "Android":
#     FINAL_TARGET = "dist/bin"` -- so clearkey is not merely compiled on
#     Android, it is deliberately packaged into the APK under lib/<arch>.
#
# Why losing it would matter (the risk flagged on LW-M1-04): the clearkey hunk is
# the back half of the librewolf.eme.gmp-clearkey.enabled story. The pref itself
# is enforced by the eme-permission split (LW-M1-03) in
# dom/media/eme/MediaKeySystemAccessManager.cpp, which decides *whether* a
# clearkey CDM may be requested; this hunk decides whether the CDM can then
# actually be created in an unsigned build. Without it,
# CreateCdmInstance() returns nullptr whenever the host verification files cannot
# be read -- i.e. always, in an unofficial build -- so a user who has turned the
# pref on still gets no clearkey. That is a silent DRM behaviour change, not a
# build error, which is why both media/ files stay in this half intact.
#
# ANDROID: no companion Android patch is needed for these two files; they are
# ordinary core sources with no mobile/ counterpart. For the browser-init
# question -- whether mobile/android has its own equivalent of the desktop hunk
# -- see the header of patches/moz-official-desktop.patch. The answer is no, and
# the evidence is recorded there and in assets/patches/android.txt.
#
diff --git a/dom/media/gmp/ChromiumCDMAdapter.cpp b/dom/media/gmp/ChromiumCDMAdapter.cpp
index 475de25..e1f0324 100644
